    id: "family-events",
    title: "Family events and blessings",
    blurb: "Talk about clans, birthdays, family gatherings, soul calling and the wrist-string blessing.",
    deck: "Family events",
    body: `
<p>Family events bring the whole extended family together. This lesson gives you the words for the people, the gatherings and some of the traditions. Families of different faiths, traditional and Christian, mark births, weddings and New Year in their own ways.</p>
<h3>Clan and family</h3>
<p>Every Hmong family belongs to a clan, <b>xeem</b> <i>creaky</i>, which is also the surname: Vang, Yang, Xiong, Lee, Thao, Vue, Moua, Her, Lor and others. Members of the same clan often think of each other as related. The family is <b>tsev neeg</b> <i>rising · falling-breathy</i>, and <b>sawvdaws</b> <i>rising · low</i> is "everyone".</p>
{{ex:Koj yog Hmoob xeem dab tsi?|falling · falling-breathy · high · creaky · high · mid|Which Hmong clan are you?}}
{{ex:Kuv yog Hmoob xeem Vang.|rising · falling-breathy · high · creaky · —|I'm from the Vang clan.}}
{{ex:Sawvdaws tuaj noj mov.|rising · low · falling · falling · rising|Everyone comes to eat.}}
<p>Watch the tone letter: <b>xeem</b> (creaky tone) is clan, but <b>xeev</b> <i>rising</i> is a US state.</p>
<h3>Celebrations</h3>
<p>A celebration or ceremony is <b>koob tsheej</b> <i>high · falling</i>. For a birthday, say <b>Zoo siab hnub yug!</b>, "Happy birthday!".</p>
{{ex:Zoo siab hnub yug!|mid · high · high · falling-breathy|Happy birthday!}}
<h3>Counting people</h3>
<p>People need a counting word too. After <b>ob</b> (two) it is written <b>tug</b>, a form of <b>tus</b>: <b>ob tug</b>.</p>
{{ex:Kuv muaj ob tug me nyuam.|rising · falling · high · falling-breathy · mid · creaky|I have two children.}}
<h3>Two traditional blessings</h3>
<p>In many families, a traditional ceremony called <b>hu plig</b> <i>mid · falling-breathy</i>, soul calling, calls a person's soul home to the body: for example for a new baby, at New Year, or after an illness or a fright. In another blessing, <b>khi tes</b> <i>mid · low</i> ("tie hand"), elders tie strings around a person's wrist and wish them health. Families name it in slightly different ways; ask yours what they call it.</p>
{{ex:Lawv hu plig.|rising · mid · falling-breathy|They are doing a soul calling.}}
{{ex:Yawg khi tes.|falling-breathy · mid · low|Grandpa ties the wrist strings.}}
<p>At any family event, thank the people who hosted and cooked: <b>ua tsaug</b>, said to the elder by name.</p>
{{ex:Pog, ua tsaug.|falling-breathy · mid · falling-breathy|Thank you, Grandma.}}
<div class="tip">Most common mistake: counting people with no counting word. <b>Kuv muaj ob me nyuam.</b> → <b>Kuv muaj ob tug me nyuam.</b></div>
`,
